1
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:33,750
This is Walnut DataBridge.

2
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:36,150
Let's start with Aspire's own question.

3
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:46,800
Two people at a fintech ask the same thing: why has Velquarry Trading's account been restricted, and what should I tell the customer?

4
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:52,800
Amy, an account manager, gets the approved customer script, with its sources, and nothing else.

5
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,200
Rachel is the money-laundering reporting officer.

6
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,600
She gets the actual case: the flagged transfers, and the review.

7
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,600
Same system, two different truths.

8
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:07,200
In Singapore, telling Amy that a suspicious-transaction report even exists is a criminal offence.

9
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,200
So the system never does.

10
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:12,400
The question is word for word the same.

11
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,200
Only who is signed in is different.

12
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:24,250
Now Ken, an outside contractor.

13
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:27,050
On the left, he asks about Velquarry.

14
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:30,650
On the right, about a company that doesn't exist.

15
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:33,450
The answers are identical, word for word.

16
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:36,250
A restricted file looks exactly like nothing.

17
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:39,850
No count, and no hint that anything is hidden.

18
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:43,450
We ran four hundred pairs like this: zero differences.

19
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:48,700
Here's Rachel's own screen.

20
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:53,100
Every sentence points at a passage she can open right now.

21
00:01:53,100 --> 00:02:01,100
Click one, and you see the exact lines, the version, and when it was checked with the source: seconds ago.

22
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:06,300
Anything she cannot open at that moment is left out of the answer.

23
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:10,300
And the screens you ask from work in Chinese too.

24
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:19,750
Now the developer side, as Maya from compliance.

25
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:26,550
The app has two modes: User, for asking, and Developer, for seeing how an answer was made.

26
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:29,350
This is Rachel's request, end to end.

27
00:02:29,350 --> 00:02:35,750
Each bar is a real timing: two permission checks first, then the answer is put together.

28
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:40,550
These three records were sealed and chained before Rachel saw a word.

29
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:45,750
And below, every document that was considered, with its verdict and the reason.

30
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:47,550
Maya sees every decision.

31
00:02:47,550 --> 00:02:49,950
She never sees the answer itself.

32
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:58,400
Maya can also ask the audit log in plain English: everything Rachel accessed in the last seven days.

33
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:01,600
She sees the filter first, then the rows.

34
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:07,200
Every question anyone asks is written to this chain before the answer is released.

35
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:10,400
Verify re-checks every link in the chain: intact.

36
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,200
Now the fun part.

37
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,200
The tamper demo copies the log and attacks the copy.

38
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:22,600
It changes one old decision, from "forbidden" to "verified", and Verify points at the exact entry.

39
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:25,800
Deleting or rolling back records is caught too.

40
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:28,200
The real log was never touched.

41
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,800
That's what we mean by answers you can prove.

42
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:38,250
From an answer to an action.

43
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:43,050
Jordan asks for a draft reply, built only from the cited facts.

44
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:46,650
A ticket is only a proposal until he confirms.

45
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:50,250
He confirms, and the ticket exists, with a link.

46
00:03:53,500 --> 00:04:01,100
Answers can become files: a table whose figures point at their sources, and a chart from the same facts.

47
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:04,300
Or a PowerPoint file, built without a model.

48
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:13,150
And the brain keeps up.

49
00:04:13,150 --> 00:04:18,750
Jordan posts a note in Slack, and it is in his graph straight away.

50
00:04:18,750 --> 00:04:23,950
Ken's graph, right beside it, shows nothing new: he cannot open that channel.

51
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:30,350
A moment later Jordan asks about the note, and the answer quotes it, with its source.

52
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:40,000
New files can be added too, into a folder, and the upload is written to the audit log.

53
00:04:42,450 --> 00:04:44,250
New here?

54
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:46,050
There's a guided tour.

55
00:04:46,050 --> 00:04:50,850
You can dictate a question; nothing is sent until you press Ask.

56
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:54,050
The value calculator lets you change every input.

57
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:58,850
And one person can run the whole stage demo from the keyboard.

58
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:05,300
Everything Jordan can open, as one graph, each item checked with its source.

59
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:12,100
Compare two people, and Ken's graph is a small corner of the company, with no greyed-out boxes.

60
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:14,900
For Ken, the rest simply isn't there.

61
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:22,950
Underneath it all is Walnut, the evidence engine.

62
00:05:22,950 --> 00:05:28,150
Drag the time slider, and the company's knowledge rewinds to any past date.

63
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:33,750
And any value can be traced to the exact row in the exact file.

64
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:45,800
Maya's analytics come straight from the verified log: how many questions, how many answered, how fast.

65
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:49,800
Seeded history is labelled, and one click leaves it out.

66
00:05:51,050 --> 00:05:55,450
The Trust centre lists the seven controls behind all of this.

67
00:05:55,450 --> 00:06:03,050
Each one is enforced in code, mapped to Singapore's AI and data rules, and proved by a named test.

68
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:08,700
We built this with Tencent CodeBuddy, nine sessions in all.

69
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:14,300
It built the WorkBuddy connectors, wrote tests, and its security review found real bugs.

70
00:06:16,350 --> 00:06:18,750
It also lives inside Tencent WorkBuddy.

71
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:25,550
Each connector carries its own signed token, so Jordan's connector gets Jordan's answer, and Ken's gets Ken's.

72
00:06:25,550 --> 00:06:27,550
Same gates, same audit log.

73
00:06:29,650 --> 00:06:34,050
The cover art was made in Miora, from a written brief.

74
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:41,700
Tencent Hy3 is wired in through TokenHub, with a safe fallback.

75
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:47,700
Until it is switched on, answers are quoted from the sources, through the same checks.

76
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:52,150
We didn't just demo it, we attacked it.

77
00:06:52,150 --> 00:06:56,150
Seven thousand three hundred and seventy-four adversarial requests: zero leaks.

78
00:06:56,150 --> 00:06:58,550
Four hundred paired questions: zero differences.

79
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:05,750
And one thousand one hundred and thirty-one backend tests, plus a hundred and sixty-six browser tests, none failing.

80
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:08,400
What's it worth?

81
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:17,200
For a six-hundred-person fintech, if each person saves ninety minutes a week, that's about one point three million US dollars a year.

82
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:19,600
It's an estimate, not a measurement.

83
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:24,000
And it pays for itself at about seven minutes a week.

84
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:28,850
Every company's AI will be asked to prove itself.

85
00:07:28,850 --> 00:07:30,650
Walnut DataBridge.

86
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:32,450
Answers you can prove.

87
00:07:32,450 --> 00:07:34,850
One pilot team, one regulated company.

88
00:07:34,850 --> 00:07:35,550
Thanks for watching!
